<header class="page-head page-head--story">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">The story</p>
    <h1 class="page-head__title">Where Canlander came from</h1>
    <p class="page-head__lede">Canadian Highlander wasn't designed by Wizards or anyone else. It started with a group of players in Victoria, BC who wanted somewhere to keep playing their old cards, and it grew from there.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="wrap">
  <ol class="chapters">
    <li class="chapter">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>1999</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Prologue · Victoria, British Columbia</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">Old cards, old friends</h2>
        <p>Victoria has always had a lot of eternal players, people who hung on to their collections instead of chasing Standard. Around 1999 a few of them started building 100-card singleton decks out of their pet cards and stuff that wasn't good enough for Extended.</p>
        <p>There was no points list and not much of a name yet. It was just a casual way to play with the cards you liked.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter__art" aria-hidden="true">{{ mtgcard("Tolarian Academy", null, "normal") }}</div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>2000s</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter one</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">The quiet years</h2>
        <p>A lot of the original group moved away for school and work, and the format went quiet for a while. When some of them came back to Victoria, they started teaching it to new people, and a few players in particular kept pushing it until there were enough people to run events.</p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter chapter--flip">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>2007</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter two · Yellowjacket Comics &amp; Toys</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">The first tournament</h2>
        <p>The first real tournament ran in 2007 at Yellowjacket Comics, which the community still thinks of as home. Once there were prizes on the line, people started building to win, and some decks got out of hand fast.</p>
        <p>Tinker was one of the early problems. Tinker into Darksteel Colossus ended games before the other player could do much, and it was clear the format needed some kind of limit.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter__art" aria-hidden="true">{{ mtgcard("Tinker", null, "normal") }}</div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>2008</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter three</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">From a ban list to a points list</h2>
        <p>About a year later the group tried a ban list. It didn't fit the format very well. A lot of people played because they wanted to use cards like Black Lotus, and banning them took that away.</p>
        <p>Over time the ban list turned into the points list. Instead of banning the strongest cards, each one got a cost, and every deck got the same budget (seven points back then) to spend however it wanted. You could play Lotus, you just couldn't play Lotus and everything else too.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter__art" aria-hidden="true">{{ mtgcard("Black Lotus", 7, "normal") }}</div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter chapter--flip">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>2014</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter four · "Highlander for Staples"</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">Playing for a Mana Drain</h2>
        <p>By 2014 the Yellowjacket events were drawing real numbers. "Highlander for Staples" that August had 28 players competing for a Mana Drain. Benjamin Wheeler won it with mono-green Craterhoof, and Robin Sorensen made top four with the blue-green tempo deck people called "UG Sorensen."</p>
        <p>Both of them are on the council today.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter__art" aria-hidden="true">{{ mtgcard("Mana Drain", 1, "normal") }}</div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>2016</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter five</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">People outside Victoria start playing</h2>
        <p>Marshall Sutcliffe from the Limited Resources podcast picked up the format from Victoria players and wrote about it in 2015. On January 1, 2016 the budget went from seven points to ten, and the biggest cards went up in price to match.</p>
        <p>That May, Wizards published Sutcliffe's introduction to Canadian Highlander on its own site. He called it the most fun Constructed format he'd ever played, and a lot of people tried it for the first time because of that article.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter__art" aria-hidden="true">{{ mtgcard("Time Walk", 5, "normal") }}</div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter chapter--flip chapter--noart">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>2017</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter six</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">North 100</h2>
        <p>LoadingReadyRun, who are also based in Victoria, started North 100, a podcast about Canadian Highlander with set reviews, deck building and points talk. It's still going, and their North 100 Showdown gameplay videos are how a lot of people learned what the format looks like. <a href="/videos/">Watch some here.</a></p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter chapter--noart">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>Online</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter seven</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">Playing online</h2>
        <p>These days a lot of Canlander happens on Discord: weekly tournaments on webcam and Cockatrice, monthly leagues that include Magic Online, and pickup games whenever people are around. Plenty of the players have never been to Victoria, and many of them now run events and write about the format themselves.</p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="chapter chapter--flip">
      <div class="chapter__year"><span>2026</span></div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <p class="chapter__kicker mono">Chapter eight</p>
        <h2 class="chapter__title">A bigger council</h2>
        <p>In January 2026 the council went from five seats to seven and voted in Cyclopes8 and Kelvin, its first members from outside Victoria, to represent the Discord and international players.</p>
        <p>In March, Time Walk and Thassa's Oracle dropped a point while The One Ring and Tamiyo went up. In September, with combo decks doing a little too well, Enlightened Tutor, Spellseeker and The Fantasticar picked up a point and Tolarian Academy went to two. Trenton M., a Victoria regular, joined the council that same month.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter__art" aria-hidden="true">{{ mtgcard("Enlightened Tutor", 1, "normal") }}</div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <section class="epilogue">
    <p class="eyebrow">Today</p>
    <h2 class="h2">Next points update: {{ site.nextPointsUpdate | longDate }}</h2>
    <p>Twenty-seven years in, the idea hasn't changed much. Almost every card is legal, every deck has the same budget, and the people who play the format decide what things cost. If you want to be part of the next chapter, the Discord runs a tournament every weekend.</p>
